1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,638
This is MATH 239, video 10b. We solve exam-type problems on compositions. Video 10a gives the method.

2
00:00:09,088 --> 00:00:24,181
The problems are: Tutorial 3, question 2, parts a to d. Assignment 3, questions 2 and 3. And question 3 of the practice midterm. For each problem, pause the video and try it first.

3
00:00:24,631 --> 00:00:41,256
The practice midterm says: define a relevant set and a weight function, and justify how you get the generating series. So a full answer has four parts. First, the set. Write it as a union of Cartesian products of part sets, and say why the union is disjoint.

4
00:00:41,706 --> 00:00:47,093
Second, the weight function. For compositions, it is usually the size, the sum of the parts.

5
00:00:47,543 --> 00:00:56,553
Third, the lemmas. Name each lemma when you use it. The Product Lemma needs the weight of a list to be the sum of the weights of its entries. The String Lemma needs no element of weight 0.

6
00:00:57,003 --> 00:01:06,105
Fourth, the answer. Write it as the coefficient of x to the n in a ratio of two polynomials, as the question asks.

7
00:01:06,555 --> 00:01:22,298
Tutorial 3, question 2a. A is the set of compositions with length congruent to 1 modulo 3. Pause the video and try it. The allowed lengths are 1, 4, 7, 10, and so on. They are purple.

8
00:01:22,748 --> 00:01:38,677
Any positive integer can be a part. So the compositions of length 3 j plus 1 form P to the 3 j plus 1. A is the union of these sets over all j. The union is disjoint, because each composition has one length.

9
00:01:39,127 --> 00:01:54,870
By the Sum Lemma, add the series of these sets. By the Product Lemma, each one is x over 1 minus x, to the power 3 j plus 1. Take out one factor. The rest is a geometric series in x over 1 minus x, cubed.

10
00:01:55,320 --> 00:02:08,463
Multiply top and bottom by 1 minus x, cubed. The answer is x minus 2 x squared plus x cubed, over 1 minus 3 x plus 3 x squared minus 2 x cubed.

11
00:02:08,913 --> 00:02:19,779
Check with n equal to 4. The composition 4 has length 1. The composition 1 1 1 1 has length 4. So the count is 2, and the series gives 2 at x to the 4.

12
00:02:20,229 --> 00:02:32,490
Question 2b. B is the set of compositions of even length with each part at most 3. Pause the video and try it. The allowed lengths are 0, 2, 4, and so on.

13
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:38,698
Step 1. The parts are 1, 2 and 3. Their series is x plus x squared plus x cubed.

14
00:02:39,148 --> 00:02:53,730
Step 2. The compositions of length 2 j form P to the 2 j. B is the disjoint union over j. By the Sum and Product Lemmas, phi of B is the sum of x plus x squared plus x cubed, to the 2 j.

15
00:02:54,180 --> 00:03:02,307
This is a geometric series in the square of phi of P. The answer is 1 over 1 minus x plus x squared plus x cubed, squared.

16
00:03:02,757 --> 00:03:13,671
Expand the square to get one fraction of polynomials. The square is x squared plus 2 x cubed plus 3 x to the 4 plus 2 x to the 5 plus x to the 6.

17
00:03:14,121 --> 00:03:28,331
Question 2c. C is the set of compositions with at least 100 parts. Pause the video and try it. Here the length has no upper bound, so we do not add 100 cases. Instead, cut each composition after part 100.

18
00:03:28,781 --> 00:03:38,301
The first piece has exactly 100 parts: it is in P to the 100. The second piece has any number of parts, zero or more: it is in P star.

19
00:03:38,751 --> 00:03:51,801
Two pieces give back the composition, and each composition gives exactly one pair. So C equals P to the 100, times P star. The size of the composition is the size of the first piece plus the size of the second piece.

20
00:03:52,251 --> 00:04:11,243
So the Product Lemma and the String Lemma apply. Phi of C is x over 1 minus x, to the 100, times 1 over 1 minus x over 1 minus x. This simplifies to x to the 100 times 1 minus x, over 1 minus x to the 100, times 1 minus 2 x.

21
00:04:11,693 --> 00:04:23,488
Question 2d. D is the set of compositions of odd length with each part at least 2. Pause the video and try it. The allowed lengths are 1, 3, 5, and so on.

22
00:04:23,938 --> 00:04:38,010
The parts are 2, 3, 4, and so on, with series x squared over 1 minus x. The compositions of length 2 j plus 1 form P to the 2 j plus 1. D is their disjoint union.

23
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:48,723
By the Sum and Product Lemmas, phi of D is the sum of phi of P to the 2 j plus 1. Take out one factor. The rest is a geometric series in phi of P squared.

24
00:04:49,173 --> 00:04:57,811
Substitute and clear the fractions. The answer is x squared minus x cubed, over 1 minus 2 x plus x squared minus x to the 4.

25
00:04:58,261 --> 00:05:11,682
Check with n equal to 6. The composition 6 has length 1, and 2 2 2 has length 3. 4 2 and 3 3 have even length, so they are not in D. The count is 2, the same as the series.

26
00:05:12,132 --> 00:05:28,943
Assignment 3, question 2a. Count the compositions of n with exactly 100 parts, all odd. Pause the video and try it. Let O be the odd positive integers. Its series is x over 1 minus x squared. The set is O to the 100.

27
00:05:29,393 --> 00:05:37,938
The weight is the sum of the parts. By the Product Lemma, the series is x to the 100 over 1 minus x squared, to the 100.

28
00:05:38,388 --> 00:05:54,921
Now find the coefficient. Divide out x to the 100: we need the coefficient of x to the n minus 100. The negative binomial series, with x squared in place of x, gives i plus 99 choose 99, times x to the 2 i.

29
00:05:55,371 --> 00:06:09,303
The coefficient row starts at x to the 100. Every odd position is 0, because 100 odd numbers have an even sum. The even positions are 1, 100, 5050, 171700.

30
00:06:09,753 --> 00:06:22,524
We need 2 i equal to n minus 100, so i is n over 2 minus 50. The answer is n over 2 plus 49, choose 99, when n is even and at least 100. Otherwise it is 0.

31
00:06:22,974 --> 00:06:38,159
Assignment 3, question 2b. The parts must alternate: part 1 odd, part 2 even, part 3 odd, and so on. Pause the video and try it. Here are the five compositions of 7 with this property.

32
00:06:38,609 --> 00:06:45,668
Odd parts are blue. Even parts are purple. Each composition starts with an odd part, and the colours alternate.

33
00:06:46,118 --> 00:06:58,193
Group the parts in pairs from the left: an odd part, then an even part. 1 2 1 2 1 is two pairs and one odd part at the end.

34
00:06:58,643 --> 00:07:11,135
So there are two cases. If the last part is odd, the composition is k pairs and then one odd part. If the last part is even, or there are no parts, it is k pairs only. The two sets are disjoint.

35
00:07:11,585 --> 00:07:29,511
The series for odd parts is x over 1 minus x squared. For even parts it is x squared over 1 minus x squared. By the Product and String Lemmas, S 2 has series 1 over 1 minus phi O times phi E. S 1 has the same series times phi O.

36
00:07:29,961 --> 00:07:43,753
By the Sum Lemma, add them: 1 plus phi O, over 1 minus phi O phi E. Multiply top and bottom by 1 minus x squared, squared. You get the fraction in the question. Its coefficient of x to the 7 is 5.

37
00:07:44,203 --> 00:08:01,433
Assignment 3, question 3. Pause the video and try it. For a n, the parts are 1, 4, 7, and so on. Their series is x over 1 minus x cubed. Any length is allowed, so the String Lemma gives 1 minus x cubed, over 1 minus x minus x cubed.

38
00:08:01,883 --> 00:08:16,883
For b n, the parts are 3, 4, 5, and so on. Their series is x cubed over 1 minus x. The String Lemma gives 1 minus x, over 1 minus x minus x cubed. Both have the same denominator.

39
00:08:17,333 --> 00:08:28,664
To compare a n with b n plus 2, multiply A by x squared. This moves its coefficients two places to the right. The two rows agree from x cubed on. The green cells show this.

40
00:08:29,114 --> 00:08:53,774
Prove it with algebra. x squared A minus B has the numerator x squared minus x to the 5, minus 1, plus x. This factors as x squared minus 1, times the denominator. So the difference is x squared minus 1. It has no terms of degree 3 or more. So a k minus 2 equals b k for k at least 3. That is, a n equals b n plus 2 for n at least 1.

41
00:08:54,224 --> 00:09:15,307
Practice midterm, question 3. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: the allowed parts are the numbers congruent to 2 or 3 mod 5: 2, 3, 7, 8, 12, 13, and so on. Their series is x squared plus x cubed, over 1 minus x to the 5.

42
00:09:15,757 --> 00:09:21,516
Step 2: the allowed lengths are 1, 6, 11, and so on. They are purple.

43
00:09:21,966 --> 00:09:44,582
So the set is the disjoint union over j of P to the 5 j plus 1. Name the weight: the size. By the Sum and Product Lemmas, the series is the sum of phi of P to the 5 j plus 1. This is phi of P over 1 minus phi of P to the 5. Phi of P has no constant term, so the geometric series is valid.

44
00:09:45,032 --> 00:10:02,122
The green row shows the coefficients. For n up to 11, only one part fits, so the count is 1 when n is 2, 3, 7 or 8 mod 5. From n equal to 12, six parts fit too. A brute force count agrees with every coefficient up to n equal to 25.

45
00:10:02,572 --> 00:10:14,321
The question forbids an infinite sum. So write the answer as one fraction: multiply top and bottom by 1 minus x to the 5, to the 5. The answer is the coefficient of x to the n in this fraction.

46
00:10:14,771 --> 00:10:20,855
Recap. Any length: the set is P star, and the series is 1 over 1 minus phi of P.

47
00:10:21,305 --> 00:10:26,738
Exactly k parts: P to the k, and the series is phi of P to the k.

48
00:10:27,188 --> 00:10:37,080
Length congruent to r mod m: the union of P to the m j plus r. The series is phi of P to the r, over 1 minus phi of P to the m.

49
00:10:37,530 --> 00:10:46,911
At least k parts: cut after part k. The set is P to the k times P star, and the series is phi of P to the k over 1 minus phi of P.

50
00:10:47,361 --> 00:10:52,005
Parts whose type alternates: group them in pairs, and add an optional last part.

51
00:10:52,455 --> 00:10:59,375
In every answer, define the set and the weight, name each lemma, and check a small n by listing the compositions.
